Observations on Columns:
Column Q(n)/n: The values are strictly increasing as n grows. This indicates that a linear
function function is a lower bound, meaning Q(n) grows faster than a line; Q(n) is in Omega(n).

Column Q(n)/n^2: The values continue to steadily increase across the entire data range. This
shows that a quadratic function is also a lower bound; Q(n) is in Omega(n^2).

Column Q(n)/n^3: The values strictly decrease toward zero as n increases. This confirms that
a cubic function serves as an upper bound; Q(n) is in O(n^3).

Column Q(n)/n^2.7: The ratios quickly stabilize and hold constant at approximately 0.00301
for the remaining data entries.


Conclusion:
The given data demonstrates that the function Q(n) is strictly bounded below by Omega(n^2) and
bounded above by O(n^3). Based on the empirical stabilization observed when scaling by n^2.7,
the data strongly suggests that Q(n) is Theta(n^2.7).